    "title": "MEP Planning Mistakes That Increase Factory Operating Costs",
    "slug": "mep-planning-mistakes-that-increase-factory-operating-costs",
    "category": "Industrial MEP & EPC",
    "excerpt": "Learn how MEP planning mistakes can increase factory operating costs and how disciplined industrial MEP planning can improve efficiency and reduce long-term expenses.",
    "content": "# Industrial Flooring Options for Manufacturing Plants\n\nChoosing flooring is typically the final task in planning a factory project, which is precisely why it can become problematic. At the point when the decision is made, the structure has already been determined, along with any slope requirements and curing times, even though the floor may be the most heavily trafficked area in the building.\n\n**Industrial flooring for factories cannot be merely a cosmetic choice; it is a decision about structural integrity and safety compliance that must be addressed alongside the project's civil engineering.**\n\nThe floor of a factory has to be able to handle forklift traffic, chemical spills, machine vibration, temperature fluctuations, and constant foot traffic for 10 years or more without being replaced.\n\n# Why This Decision Belongs Earlier in the Project\n\nThe flooring is not an independent finishing but is linked with the sub-base, drainage system, expansion joints, and the curing period of the entire building.\n\nSelecting the **industrial flooring for factories** in conjunction with the total EPC and civil work scope, rather than as an add-on at the end, will ensure that the sub-base is properly compacted, the moisture barrier is properly laid, and time for curing is factored into the schedule.\n\nThe floors hurriedly done to meet the deadline are the ones that develop cracks and delamination within a year.\n\n# The Major Flooring Types Used in Manufacturing\n\n# Epoxy Resin Flooring\n\nEpoxy resin flooring is one of the most widely used flooring types in manufacturing. Epoxy resin can be installed on concrete as a seamless flooring system, providing good chemical resistance, easy maintenance, and durability.\n\n![industrial flooring for factories.avif](https://pub-eeb28a3c927b4ae1b67e3e8e731ee105.r2.dev/industrial_flooring_for_factories_32e837e8a8.avif)\n\nThis type of flooring is suitable for areas such as automotive assembly shops, food processing facilities, and pharmaceutical plants where chemical resistance and cleanliness are required.\n\nA disadvantage of epoxy resin flooring is the time-consuming application and proper substrate preparation; otherwise, the epoxy will peel off within a few months.\n\n# Polished and Hardened Concrete\n\nPolished and hardened concrete is a relatively economical material that provides a very durable floor, suitable for heavy manufacturing and warehouse applications.\n\nFirst, the concrete floor is treated with hardening compounds, then it is polished. Polishing and hardening of concrete result in an abrasion-resistant, dense floor suitable for the movement of forklifts and heavy equipment.\n\nThis type of flooring is less resistant to chemicals than epoxy resin flooring.\n\n# Vitrified and Anti-Skid Tiles\n\nVitrified and anti-skid tiles are suitable where hygiene, aesthetic appearance, and non-slip characteristics need to be combined, such as in quality control laboratories, packing zones, and staff zones.\n\nThey are more costly to install and maintain than poured systems and are not commonly used on the production line itself, although there are justifiable reasons for their use in particular zones.\n\n# Anti-Static Flooring\n\nAnti-static flooring is a specialised flooring system designed for electronics manufacturing and situations where static discharge could damage equipment components or pose a fire risk.\n\nIt has conductive components that allow dissipation of static charge and is important in facilities where electronics or certain chemical processes are manufactured.\n\n# Heavy-Duty Industrial Screed\n\nA heavy-duty industrial screed is installed in areas requiring high point loads or machinery bases.\n\nThis type of flooring is often installed in heavy engineering plants and facilities such as copper smelting plants.\n\n# Matching Flooring to the Actual Use Case\n\nIt’s less a matter of preference than it is about what the floor has to endure daily.\n\nFor high-load and forklift traffic, polished concrete or a reinforced screed is better suited than coated flooring because it can withstand impact without damaging the surface.\n\nFor chemical exposure and food-grade standards, epoxy resin flooring is often preferred because it is seamless, non-porous, and resistant to cleaning chemicals and oily or acidic substances.\n\nAnti-static flooring is essential where controlling electrostatic discharge is a critical requirement, such as electronics manufacturing.\n\nNone of these options need to be mutually exclusive in one facility.\n\nIt is not unusual for a plant to use epoxy flooring in its production areas, hardened concrete in its warehousing areas, and anti-static flooring in the electronics assembly area all in the same building.\n\n# Where Cross-Sector Experience Matters\n\nIndustrial flooring requirements differ greatly from industry to industry, and a contractor with experience working only within one industry may apply the same specification regardless of its suitability.\n\nPISL has delivered more than 16 million square feet of industrial infrastructure since 2009, across sectors including automotive, warehousing, heavy engineering, industrial manufacturing, food processing, and copper smelting.\n\nThis cross-sector experience covers a wide range of industrial flooring requirements, from hygiene-focused flooring in food processing plants to heavy-duty floors for engineering applications.\n\nThere is no one-size-fits-all industrial factory floor specification.\n\nWhile a particular flooring specification may be suitable for warehousing, the same solution may be inappropriate for food processing or electronics manufacturing.\n\n# Planning Flooring as Part of the Bigger Picture\n\nOne of the most easily prevented mistakes in industrial construction is treating flooring in factory buildings as an afterthought.\n\nCorrecting a failed floor can involve partial demolition and refitting while the manufacturing process is ongoing.\n\nPlanning the industrial flooring system in parallel with the other civil and structural components, based on the actual load and hygiene needs of the facility from the beginning, is clearly the more economical and efficient approach.\n\nWhen designing a new manufacturing facility or replacing flooring in an existing one, the PISL team can help determine which industrial flooring specification best suits your industry's loads, operational requirements and compliance needs as part of the broader facility design.",
